From f9a5237e0288c5389019ec4b9ec77c61a2a86354 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 15:57:47 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: patch 0144 -- OpenDevice bisection diagnostic (S7)

WHY: S7's investigation has ruled out eight hypotheses (poll cost,
poll multiplier, regwrite cost, yield-switch volume, DMA-cycle-stealing,
audio-thread existence, IRQ7/LPT1 sharing, the whole per-interrupt-
servicing-cost family) for the ~9.7x-11.8x uniform per-UpdateGameOnce()
tax that appears whenever the SB16/PicoGUS device is open on HW-486-66,
regardless of DMA activity, thread existence, or interrupt fire count.
What's left: a one-time OpenDevice side effect, not an ongoing cost.

WHAT: SDL_HINT_DOS_AUDIO_OPEN_STOP_AT=1|2|3|4 makes OpenDevice return
successfully at one of four points along its own real hardware-bring-up
sequence -- before the DMA controller is touched (1), after it's
programmed+unmasked but before DOS_HookInterrupt (2), after the IRQ
vector is hooked but before any DSP command (3), or after the DSP
speaker-enable (0xD1) but before autoinit playback actually starts (4)
-- so a real-hardware A/B can bisect exactly which piece of the
sequence the tax first appears at. DMA never actually engages in any of
the four phases (the autoinit-start command never runs), so this is
purely about setup-time state, not transfer activity.

Also relocates the ring/staging-buffer allocation (chunk_size/ring_size/
ring_buffer/staging_buffer, isr_* mirrors, SDL/0107 priming, SDL/0108
witness) earlier in OpenDevice, before phase 1's stop point -- verified
it depends only on hidden->dma_channel/dma_buffer/dma_buflen/is_16bit
and device->spec.* (all already set), and nothing between its old and
new position depends on it either. Needed because audio_core_entry_
poll()'s existing per-tick SDL_DOSAudioPump() call keeps running
regardless of where OpenDevice stopped; without this move, phases 1-2
would leave GetDeviceBuf()/PlayDevice() operating on a NULL ring buffer,
a real crash risk. ISR memory-locking (DOS_LockCode/DOS_LockData/
DOS_LockVariable) deliberately stays at its original later position --
phases 1-2 stop before reaching it, which is safe because the IRQ is
never hooked in those phases either, so no interrupt context ever
touches that memory.

Verified DOS_UnhookInterrupt() (SDL_dos.c) already no-ops safely on a
never-hooked (calloc-zeroed) hook structure before relying on it for
phases 1-2's teardown -- read the implementation, not assumed.

Strict-integer match ("1".."4"). Default OFF preserves current
behavior bit-identical. Diagnostic only -- expect total silence in
every phase.

Temporary -- to be reverted once this investigation concludes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c | 241 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 187 insertions(+), 54 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
index 0f09c5ff8..0d9a8e0d1 100644
--- a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
+++ b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
@@ -822,6 +822,49 @@ volatile bool g_dos_audio_force_dsp_pause = false;
    when the hint is exactly "1". */
 static bool g_dos_audio_no_bg_thread = false;
 
+/* DOS-PORT patch 0144: OpenDevice bisection diagnostic (S7).
+   S7-irq7-lpt1-hypothesis-refuted-2026-09-14.md: what's now common to
+   every collapsed real-hardware arm (thread present/absent, DMA active/
+   halted, IRQ firing 0 to ~2000 times) and absent only when the device
+   is fully closed is that OpenDevice ran at all -- some ONE-TIME side
+   effect established during OpenDevice, not any ongoing servicing or
+   interrupt-frequency cost (already independently disproven: Arm B/Arm D
+   show the same tax at 0 vs ~2000 real IRQ fires). This diagnostic lets
+   OpenDevice stop early at one of four points along its own real
+   hardware-bring-up sequence, so a real-hardware A/B can find exactly
+   which piece of that sequence the tax first appears at:
+
+     SDL_HINT_DOS_AUDIO_OPEN_STOP_AT=1 -- stop right after the DMA buffer
+       is allocated + silenced, BEFORE the 8237 DMA controller is touched
+       at all, before DOS_HookInterrupt, before any DSP command beyond
+       the unconditional ResetSoundBlasterDSP() every open already does.
+     =2 -- stop right after the 8237 DMA controller channel is
+       programmed (mode set) AND unmasked, still before DOS_HookInterrupt
+       and before any DSP command.
+     =3 -- stop right after DOS_HookInterrupt (IRQ vector installed, PIC
+       line unmasked), still before any DSP command.
+     =4 -- stop right after WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xD1) (speaker enabled)
+       and its completion wait, still before the DSP is ever told to
+       actually start autoinit playback (0x1C/8-bit or 0xB6/16-bit) --
+       so DMA never actually engages in ANY of these four phases; the
+       question is purely about setup-time state, not transfer activity.
+
+   Each stop point returns true (a normal, successful SDL open) so the
+   engine proceeds exactly as it would with real audio -- ring/mixer
+   registered, audio_core_entry_poll() runs every tick exactly like a
+   real device, just silently. Safe to tear down at any of the four
+   points: CloseDevice's own DOS_UnhookInterrupt already no-ops on a
+   never-hooked (calloc-zeroed, hook->fn == NULL) structure
+   (SDL_dos.c's own `if (!hook || !hook->fn) return;`), the DMA-channel
+   mask-at-close is an idempotent port write regardless of prior arm
+   state, and hidden->dma_buffer/ring_buffer/staging_buffer are each
+   already NULL-guarded there -- verified by reading CloseDevice in full
+   before adding this, not assumed. Strict-integer match ("1".."4";
+   anything else, including unset, is the normal full open). Default
+   OFF preserves current behavior bit-identical. Diagnostic only --
+   expect total silence in every phase (DMA truly never starts). */
+static int g_dos_audio_open_stop_at = 0;
+
 // ISR-visible ring buffer state (all memory-locked).
 static volatile int isr_ring_read = 0;
 static volatile int isr_ring_write = 0;
@@ -2055,6 +2098,23 @@ static bool DOSSOUNDBLASTER_OpenDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
 {
     const bool is_sb16 = soundblaster_is_sb16;
 
+    /* DOS-PORT patch 0144: resolve the OpenDevice-bisection stop phase.
+       See g_dos_audio_open_stop_at's own declaration comment for the
+       full rationale. Strict-integer match against "1".."4"; anything
+       else (unset, "0", malformed) leaves it 0 (normal full open). */
+    {
+        const char *e = SDL_getenv("SDL_HINT_DOS_AUDIO_OPEN_STOP_AT");
+        g_dos_audio_open_stop_at = 0;
+        if (e && e[0] >= '1' && e[0] <= '4' && e[1] == 0) {
+            g_dos_audio_open_stop_at = e[0] - '0';
+            dos_port_dbg_log("audio: SDL_HINT_DOS_AUDIO_OPEN_STOP_AT=%d -- "
+                             "OpenDevice will stop early at phase %d; "
+                             "diagnostic only, expect total silence "
+                             "(DMA never actually starts in any phase)",
+                             g_dos_audio_open_stop_at, g_dos_audio_open_stop_at);
+        }
+    }
+
     /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11 patch 0067): capture the device pointer
        so the engine-callable SDL_DOSAudioPump() (declared in
        SDL3/SDL_dosaudio_pump.h) knows which device to iterate. Cleared
@@ -2793,60 +2853,32 @@ static bool DOSSOUNDBLASTER_OpenDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
     // silence the DMA buffer to start
     SDL_memset(hidden->dma_buffer, soundblaster_silence_value, hidden->dma_buflen);
 
-    // set up DMA controller.
-    const Uint32 physical = DOS_LinearToPhysical(hidden->dma_buffer);
-    const Uint8 physical_page = (physical >> 16) & 0xFF;
-
-    if (is_sb16) {
-        // High DMA (16-bit, channels 5-7): ports in 0xC0-0xDF range, counts in words.
-        const int dma_words = (hidden->dma_buflen / 2) - 1;
-        outportb(0xD4, 0x04 | hidden->dma_channel);                                           // mask the DMA channel
-        outportb(0xD6, 0x58 | (hidden->dma_channel - 4));                                     // mode: single, read, auto-init
-        static const int high_page_ports[] = { 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0x8B, 0x89, 0x8A };             // DMA page register ports for channels 5-7
-        outportb(high_page_ports[hidden->dma_channel], physical_page);                        // page to transfer
-        outportb(0xD8, 0x00);                                                                 // clear the flip-flop
-        outportb(0xC0 + (hidden->dma_channel - 4) * 4, (Uint8)((physical >> 1) & 0xFF));      // offset low (word address)
-        outportb(0xC0 + (hidden->dma_channel - 4) * 4, (Uint8)((physical >> 9) & 0xFF));      // offset high
-        outportb(0xD8, 0x00);                                                                 // clear the flip-flop
-        outportb(0xC0 + (hidden->dma_channel - 4) * 4 + 2, (Uint8)(dma_words & 0xFF));        // count low
-        outportb(0xC0 + (hidden->dma_channel - 4) * 4 + 2, (Uint8)((dma_words >> 8) & 0xFF)); // count high
-        outportb(0xD4, hidden->dma_channel & ~4);                                             // unmask the DMA channel
-    } else {
-        // Low DMA (8-bit, channels 0-3): ports in 0x00-0x0F range, counts in bytes.
-        static const int page_ports[] = { 0x87, 0x83, 0x81, 0x82 }; // DMA page register ports for channels 0-3 (yes, they're out of order — that's how the IBM PC DMA controller works)
-        const int dma_bytes = hidden->dma_buflen - 1;
-        outportb(0x0A, 0x04 | hidden->dma_channel);                              // mask the DMA channel
-        outportb(0x0B, 0x58 | hidden->dma_channel);                              // mode: single, read, auto-init
-        outportb(page_ports[hidden->dma_channel], physical_page);                // page to transfer
-        outportb(0x0C, 0x00);                                                    // clear the flip-flop
-        outportb(hidden->dma_channel * 2, (Uint8)(physical & 0xFF));             // offset low (byte address)
-        outportb(hidden->dma_channel * 2, (Uint8)((physical >> 8) & 0xFF));      // offset high
-        outportb(0x0C, 0x00);                                                    // clear the flip-flop
-        outportb(hidden->dma_channel * 2 + 1, (Uint8)(dma_bytes & 0xFF));        // count low
-        outportb(hidden->dma_channel * 2 + 1, (Uint8)((dma_bytes >> 8) & 0xFF)); // count high
-        outportb(0x0A, hidden->dma_channel);                                     // unmask the DMA channel (just the channel number, no bit 2)
-    }
-
-    // Cache the IRQ ack port so the ISR doesn't chase pointers.
-    isr_irq_ack_port = is_sb16 ? (soundblaster_base_port + 0x0F) : (soundblaster_base_port + 0x0E);
-
-    /* DOSKUTSU patch (SDL/0106): OpenDevice DMA-path diagnostic. Records the
-       path actually committed (chosen path, programmed DMA channel + page/
-       offset/count register family, resolved sample format + channels + rate,
-       IRQ + ack-port), so the PicoGUS-SB iter log shows exactly what the DMA
-       controller was programmed with. The DSP command sequence is logged
-       separately in each start branch below (SDL/0106). Always-on (read-only
-       snapshot at open; no killswitch). */
-    dos_port_dbg_log("audio: SDL/0106 OpenDevice DMA programmed: path=%s "
-                     "dma_ch=%d (%s) dma_buflen=%d fmt=0x%X ch=%d freq=%d Hz "
-                     "irq=%d ack_port=0x%X silence=0x%02X",
-                     is_sb16 ? "16-bit-high-DMA" : "8-bit-low-DMA",
-                     hidden->dma_channel,
-                     is_sb16 ? "high/word ports 0xC0-0xDF" : "low/byte ports 0x00-0x0F",
-                     (int)hidden->dma_buflen,
-                     (unsigned)device->spec.format, device->spec.channels,
-                     device->spec.freq, soundblaster_irq,
-                     (unsigned)isr_irq_ack_port, soundblaster_silence_value);
+    /* DOS-PORT patch 0144: the ring-buffer setup block below (chunk_size/
+       ring_size/ring_buffer/staging_buffer allocation, isr_* mirror
+       assignment, SDL/0107 8-bit silence priming, SDL/0108 format
+       witness) is RELOCATED here from its original position (after DMA
+       controller setup, before the ISR memory-locking section) so that
+       ALL FOUR phase-1..4 bisection stop points below have a fully
+       valid, allocated ring/staging buffer -- otherwise
+       audio_core_entry_poll()'s already-existing per-tick
+       SDL_DOSAudioPump() call (unaffected by this diagnostic; the
+       engine has no idea OpenDevice stopped early) would call
+       GetDeviceBuf()/PlayDevice() against a NULL hidden->ring_buffer /
+       zero hidden->chunk_size, a real crash risk -- checked and fixed
+       before touching real hardware, not assumed safe. Nothing in this
+       block depends on DMA-controller register state or the IRQ hook
+       (only on hidden->dma_channel/dma_buffer/dma_buflen/is_16bit and
+       device->spec.*, all already set above), and nothing in the DMA-
+       controller-programming section below depends on ring-buffer
+       state either -- verified by reading both sides before moving.
+       ISR memory-LOCKING (DOS_LockCode/DOS_LockData/DOS_LockVariable)
+       deliberately stays at its ORIGINAL later position (still before
+       DOS_HookInterrupt, unchanged): phases 1-2 stop before reaching
+       it, leaving the ring genuinely unlocked, which is fine because
+       the IRQ is never hooked in those phases either -- no interrupt
+       context ever touches this memory, so DPMI's normal (non-locked)
+       paging is safe. Phases 3-4 and the full normal open still reach
+       the locking code exactly as before. */
 
     // Set up the IRQ-driven ring buffer.
     hidden->chunk_size = device->buffer_size; // one DMA half-buffer
@@ -2941,6 +2973,83 @@ static bool DOSSOUNDBLASTER_OpenDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
                                         "U8 centered on 0x80 (SDL/0108 fix)");
     }
 
+    /* DOS-PORT patch 0144, phase 1: stop before the 8237 DMA controller
+       is touched at all -- before DOS_HookInterrupt, before any DSP
+       command beyond the unconditional ResetSoundBlasterDSP() above.
+       Ring/staging buffer are now already valid (relocated above), so
+       audio_core_entry_poll()'s per-tick pump call has something safe
+       to operate on even at this earliest phase. */
+    if (g_dos_audio_open_stop_at == 1) {
+        g_dos_sb_device_open = true;
+        return true;
+    }
+
+    // set up DMA controller.
+    const Uint32 physical = DOS_LinearToPhysical(hidden->dma_buffer);
+    const Uint8 physical_page = (physical >> 16) & 0xFF;
+
+    if (is_sb16) {
+        // High DMA (16-bit, channels 5-7): ports in 0xC0-0xDF range, counts in words.
+        const int dma_words = (hidden->dma_buflen / 2) - 1;
+        outportb(0xD4, 0x04 | hidden->dma_channel);                                           // mask the DMA channel
+        outportb(0xD6, 0x58 | (hidden->dma_channel - 4));                                     // mode: single, read, auto-init
+        static const int high_page_ports[] = { 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0x8B, 0x89, 0x8A };             // DMA page register ports for channels 5-7
+        outportb(high_page_ports[hidden->dma_channel], physical_page);                        // page to transfer
+        outportb(0xD8, 0x00);                                                                 // clear the flip-flop
+        outportb(0xC0 + (hidden->dma_channel - 4) * 4, (Uint8)((physical >> 1) & 0xFF));      // offset low (word address)
+        outportb(0xC0 + (hidden->dma_channel - 4) * 4, (Uint8)((physical >> 9) & 0xFF));      // offset high
+        outportb(0xD8, 0x00);                                                                 // clear the flip-flop
+        outportb(0xC0 + (hidden->dma_channel - 4) * 4 + 2, (Uint8)(dma_words & 0xFF));        // count low
+        outportb(0xC0 + (hidden->dma_channel - 4) * 4 + 2, (Uint8)((dma_words >> 8) & 0xFF)); // count high
+        outportb(0xD4, hidden->dma_channel & ~4);                                             // unmask the DMA channel
+    } else {
+        // Low DMA (8-bit, channels 0-3): ports in 0x00-0x0F range, counts in bytes.
+        static const int page_ports[] = { 0x87, 0x83, 0x81, 0x82 }; // DMA page register ports for channels 0-3 (yes, they're out of order — that's how the IBM PC DMA controller works)
+        const int dma_bytes = hidden->dma_buflen - 1;
+        outportb(0x0A, 0x04 | hidden->dma_channel);                              // mask the DMA channel
+        outportb(0x0B, 0x58 | hidden->dma_channel);                              // mode: single, read, auto-init
+        outportb(page_ports[hidden->dma_channel], physical_page);                // page to transfer
+        outportb(0x0C, 0x00);                                                    // clear the flip-flop
+        outportb(hidden->dma_channel * 2, (Uint8)(physical & 0xFF));             // offset low (byte address)
+        outportb(hidden->dma_channel * 2, (Uint8)((physical >> 8) & 0xFF));      // offset high
+        outportb(0x0C, 0x00);                                                    // clear the flip-flop
+        outportb(hidden->dma_channel * 2 + 1, (Uint8)(dma_bytes & 0xFF));        // count low
+        outportb(hidden->dma_channel * 2 + 1, (Uint8)((dma_bytes >> 8) & 0xFF)); // count high
+        outportb(0x0A, hidden->dma_channel);                                     // unmask the DMA channel (just the channel number, no bit 2)
+    }
+
+    /* DOS-PORT patch 0144, phase 2: stop right after the 8237 DMA
+       controller channel is programmed (mode set) and unmasked, still
+       before DOS_HookInterrupt and before any DSP command. The DMA
+       controller will never actually transfer anything (nothing ever
+       asserts DREQ -- the DSP is never told to start), so masking it
+       again at CloseDevice is a safe, idempotent port write regardless. */
+    if (g_dos_audio_open_stop_at == 2) {
+        g_dos_sb_device_open = true;
+        return true;
+    }
+
+    // Cache the IRQ ack port so the ISR doesn't chase pointers.
+    isr_irq_ack_port = is_sb16 ? (soundblaster_base_port + 0x0F) : (soundblaster_base_port + 0x0E);
+
+    /* DOSKUTSU patch (SDL/0106): OpenDevice DMA-path diagnostic. Records the
+       path actually committed (chosen path, programmed DMA channel + page/
+       offset/count register family, resolved sample format + channels + rate,
+       IRQ + ack-port), so the PicoGUS-SB iter log shows exactly what the DMA
+       controller was programmed with. The DSP command sequence is logged
+       separately in each start branch below (SDL/0106). Always-on (read-only
+       snapshot at open; no killswitch). */
+    dos_port_dbg_log("audio: SDL/0106 OpenDevice DMA programmed: path=%s "
+                     "dma_ch=%d (%s) dma_buflen=%d fmt=0x%X ch=%d freq=%d Hz "
+                     "irq=%d ack_port=0x%X silence=0x%02X",
+                     is_sb16 ? "16-bit-high-DMA" : "8-bit-low-DMA",
+                     hidden->dma_channel,
+                     is_sb16 ? "high/word ports 0xC0-0xDF" : "low/byte ports 0x00-0x0F",
+                     (int)hidden->dma_buflen,
+                     (unsigned)device->spec.format, device->spec.channels,
+                     device->spec.freq, soundblaster_irq,
+                     (unsigned)isr_irq_ack_port, soundblaster_silence_value);
+
     // Lock all ISR code and data to prevent page faults during interrupts.
     DOS_LockCode(SoundBlasterIRQHandler, SoundBlasterIRQHandler_End);
     DOS_LockCode(RingCopyOut, RingCopyOut_End);
@@ -3034,6 +3143,19 @@ static bool DOSSOUNDBLASTER_OpenDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
 
     DOS_HookInterrupt(soundblaster_irq, SoundBlasterIRQHandler, &hidden->interrupt_hook);
 
+    /* DOS-PORT patch 0144, phase 3: stop right after the IRQ vector is
+       installed and the PIC line unmasked, still before any DSP
+       command. IRQ will never actually fire (DMA is armed but the DSP
+       is never told to start), so this exercises "interrupt line
+       hooked and unmasked, otherwise idle" in isolation. Safe to tear
+       down: CloseDevice's DOS_UnhookInterrupt() call will actually
+       have real state to unhook this time (hook->fn is now set), the
+       normal, already-exercised path. */
+    if (g_dos_audio_open_stop_at == 3) {
+        g_dos_sb_device_open = true;
+        return true;
+    }
+
     WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0xD1); // turn on the speaker
     // The speaker-on command takes up to 112 ms to complete on real hardware.
     // Poll the DSP write status port (bit 7 clears when the DSP is ready);
@@ -3046,6 +3168,17 @@ static bool DOSSOUNDBLASTER_OpenDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
         }
     }
 
+    /* DOS-PORT patch 0144, phase 4: stop right after the DSP speaker-
+       enable (0xD1) completes, still before the DSP is ever told to
+       actually start autoinit playback (0x1C 8-bit / 0xB6 16-bit) --
+       so DMA never engages in ANY of these four phases, this one
+       included. This is the furthest point before the DSP's own
+       playback state machine actually engages. */
+    if (g_dos_audio_open_stop_at == 4) {
+        g_dos_sb_device_open = true;
+        return true;
+    }
+
     if (is_sb16) {
         // SB16 (DSP >= 4): set output sample rate directly
         WriteSoundBlasterDSP(0x41); // set output sampling rate
-- 
